From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH_MIB=<N>: ASTC to BC1/BC3 conversion through at most N MiB of scratch, in layer batches (Lichii #1083) or row bands, same output bytes; EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH_VERIFY=1 compares batched with whole; off by default

Layer batches are a port of zortos293/Lichii #1083 (open, by marcochiusso-lgtm, head d4f48aad92,
"vulkan: decode an ASTC array a few layers at a time when it does not fit"; not compiled or run
upstream). The row bands, the bounded output buffer, the CPU bands and VERIFY are new.

Where the scratch is on T88. TranscodeAstcToBcn decodes the whole texture into one RGBA8 image
(four bytes a texel, every level and layer), then encodes all of it into one BC1/BC3 buffer.
TranscodeScratchTooLarge (EDEN_NX2_GPU_TRANSCODE_MIB, 128 unset) sends textures over it to the CPU
path, which already decodes one slice at a time. The recent runs set GPU_TRANSCODE_MIB=256:
- the 1365x1365x90 arrays (853 MiB decode image) stay on the CPU path and TEX_TRIM_MIB=64 trims
  them there. #1083's 890 MiB image does not exist in our build;
- the single-layer 6144x6144 ASTC 5x5 ground texture (13 levels) goes to the GPU path: a 192 MiB
  decode image (144 MiB of it level 0) plus a 48 MiB BC3 output. Runs 739, 742 and 743 each made
  it 3 times ("Image 6144x6144x1 (13 levels, 1 layers, format 51) is over the memory budget";
  format 51 is the decode format). Each time "Out of device memory: keeping N MiB more free"
  followed within 0.2 s. Layer batches cannot split it. Row bands can.
PS5_Lichii's patches (0002, 0027, 0202-0211) have no converter of their own: 0002 caps an
integrated GPU's budget, which keeps ASTC re-compressed, and 0027 retries staging allocations.
Fritchii v5.5's TranscodeAstcToBcn is the whole-texture path, as ours. Nothing to port there.

EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH_MIB=<N> (1..4096, digits only; it is a size, so it is not read with PeSwitch;
unset, 0 or anything else: off). Routing does not change: WillUseGpuBcnTranscode and
TranscodeScratchTooLarge are untouched, so TEX_TRIM keeps working on the CPU-path arrays.
- GPU path: a texture whose decode image plus BC output pass N MiB is planned (PlanAstcBatches).
  If one layer with all its levels fits, it goes in layer batches: the decode image is the
  texture's own shape with floor(N / per-layer cost) layers, and each batch is the same ImageInfo
  with fewer layers, read from map.offset + base * layer_stride (#1083's reasoning: the swizzles
  do not depend on the layer count). Otherwise it goes in row bands: one layer and one level at a
  time, through a decode image as wide as level 0 with one level and one layer. A band starts on a
  row of GOB blocks (8 << block height tile rows): its input is bound at block_row * block_size,
  so the decoder's addressing from there on is the whole level's less that offset. Its height is
  a multiple of 8, so it is whole BC blocks. Only the last band ends with the level, where the
  whole path's edge is. Levels that fit go in one piece. If not even one row of GOB blocks fits,
  the texture stays whole (old path) and that is logged.
- One output buffer of the largest batch's size, reused. Each batch: decode (from the second
  batch on, the decode barrier waits for the previous encode to read the decode image), barrier
  copy->encode, encode, barrier encode->copy, then Image::UploadMemory for that batch's
  levels/layers or rows (BC copies at y multiples of 4). Before the first batch,
  TransitionImageLayout moves the image out of UNDEFINED once, so no batch's copy throws away
  another's. Everything goes into the current tick, so the staging pool frees the output only
  after the last copy. The decode image goes into pending_transcodes with its own shape. That
  keeps TickFrame, EDEN_NX2_TRANSCODE_KEEP_MIB and free_transcodes reuse right, and it is reused
  when the GPU is past it, as before. Then one Flush, as the old path's AssembleInto does once per
  texture, unless HoldSubmissionForZpass. Refusals behave as before: no decode image, or an
  unbacked one with TRANSCODE_WAIT, or no output, gives upload_refused.
- With EDEN_PE_TRANSCODE_BOUND, a batched texture counts only its decode image as incoming.
- CPU path (ConvertImage, the async decode): a slice whose RGBA8 scratch passes N MiB goes in
  bands of rows. A band is a multiple of both the ASTC block height and 4, so Decompress and
  CompressBC1/3 see the same blocks and edges and write the same bytes. On T88 with
  GPU_TRANSCODE_MIB=256 this path is idle for Ocarina (the arrays' slices are 7 MiB, or 1.8 MiB
  trimmed). It covers the 6144 texture's 144 MiB level-0 slice when the threshold is the default.

Expected for the 6144 texture at N=64 (block height 16 GOBs): level 0 in 4 bands of up to 1920
rows, level 1 in 2, the 11 smaller levels whole, so 17 batches. Peak about 45 MiB decode image
plus 11.25 MiB output (5.6 MiB if BC1) instead of 240 MiB. With 32-GOB blocks: 1280-row bands,
37.5 MiB. At N=32 a 32-GOB texture stays whole, so test N=64 first. The 3072x3072 (60 MiB) and
smaller decode images in the same runs fit 64 and do not change. N=32 would band them too. With
TRANSCODE_KEEP_MIB=32 a decode image over 32 MiB is dropped after use and made again for the next
texture (about 263 us), as before.

Log lines (Render_Vulkan, first 32, then each power of two):
"EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH: <addr> WxHxL (n levels, BC3|BC1): B batches of up to R rows|K layers, peak
scratch X MiB (decode image D, output O) instead of F MiB, recorded in T us (C so far)". D is the
driver's size for the decode image. O is what was asked of the staging pool, which may round it
up (powers of two, or 8 MiB steps with EDEN_UP_STAGING_STEPS). T is the GPU thread's recording
time. CPU path (HW_GPU): "EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH: CPU WxH level l (s slices): b bands of R rows, peak
scratch X MiB instead of Y MiB, T us". Textures that stay whole: "... needs F MiB to convert, over
N MiB, and stays whole: <reason>".

EDEN_UP_ASTC_BATCH_VERIFY=1 (PeSwitch): the first texture in layer batches and the first in row
bands (8 tries each while memory is refused) are also converted whole. That is the old decode
into a full decode image and the old encode into a readable buffer. Each batch's output is also
copied into a second readable buffer at its final place. Then one Finish (VERIFY only) and a
byte compare: "the B row bands|layer batches wrote the same X bytes as the whole conversion (GPU
wait T us)", or an error with the count of bytes that differ and the first one's level and layer.
It needs the whole decode image and twice the output on top, so on the console verify with N=8:
the first batched textures are then small ones.

Frontend (prepare-vulkan-port.py): no anchor changes. The TranscodeAstcToBcn function-head
anchor, the single "pending_transcodes.push_back(std::move(pending));" and the include anchor
each still occur once, and the wait-site count stays 2 (the VERIFY Finish line carries a
comment). The mkw_transcode timer covers the batched path, which is called from inside
TranscodeAstcToBcn. The PS5 memory-short pending wait after the old push_back does not run for a
batched texture, whose scratch is bounded instead. The next whole-path texture counts the
batched decode image in it.

Known deviations and risks. When a level is narrower than level 0 in band mode, the decoder's
padding threads (up to 7 tiles past a row) decode into the wider image instead of stopping at its
edge. The encoder never reads there. Their reads stay inside the level's GOB padding, except at
the very end of the guest data, where RADV's buffer bounds check returns zero. The batched path
gives more barriers and dispatches for one texture (17 batches for the ground texture), so it
can take more GPU time than one big dispatch. It does not add to AssembleInto's per-5-s decode
count or time statistics. Not ported from #1083: halving the batch after a failed full-size
allocation. Here the budget decides before allocating. Not built, not run: git apply --check
passes on T88 and on T88 with 0495's util hunks; checked by reading only.

Unset, or anything but 1..4096 digits: PlanAstcBatches is not called, TranscodeAstcToBcn and the
BOUND block run as before, and ConvertImage takes its old branch. That branch's statements are
re-indented inside "if (band_rows == 0)" with no other change. VERIFY does nothing without
BATCH_MIB.

